options(ospca.threads = 2, width = 80)
@

\section{Introduction}

For comparison with the one-sided methods of the package, \code{gspca()}
implements the independent nonlinear component analysis of Gunsilius and
Schennach (2023), which we call GS-PCA. Unlike \code{ospca()} and
\code{nlospca()}, it is a bidirectional method.

Let $X$ be a random vector in $\mathbb{R}^d$ with density $f$, and let $T$ be
the Brenier map that pushes the distribution of $X$ forward to the $d$-variate
standard normal distribution, with density $\varphi$. That is, $T$ is the
gradient of a convex function and $T(X)$ is standard normal. We write $y$ for
points in the space of the data and $x = T(y)$ for points in the space of the
normal reference distribution, and $J_g$ for the Jacobian matrix of a map $g$.
Since $T$ is the gradient of a convex function, $J_T$ is symmetric and positive
definite. The directions $u_1, \ldots, u_d$ are the eigenvectors, in decreasing
order of the eigenvalues, of
\[
\bar J = \int \varphi(x) \ln J_{T^{-1}}(x) \, dx
       = -\int f(y) \ln J_T(y) \, dy,
\]
where $\ln$ denotes the matrix logarithm, and the two forms are equal as
$J_{T^{-1}}(x) = J_T(T^{-1}(x))^{-1}$. The principal curves are
$T^{-1}(r u_j)$, $r \in \mathbb{R}$, the scores are
$\langle T(X), u_j \rangle$, and the $k$-dimensional representation of $X$ is
\[
T^{-1}\Big(\sum_{j \le k} \langle T(X), u_j \rangle u_j\Big).
\]
For Gaussian data, $T^{-1}$ is linear, the directions are the linear principal
components, and the eigenvalues of $\bar J$ are half the logarithms of the
eigenvalues of the covariance matrix.

The implementation is a simple discrete estimator and not the smooth potential
fit of the paper. A tensor grid of $n_g$ = \code{n\_grid} standard normal
quantiles per axis is coupled with the sample by an exact optimal transport
plan, and the image of a grid point under $T^{-1}$ is the barycenter of the
observations coupled with it. The Jacobians of $T^{-1}$ are then estimated at
the grid points by symmetrized central differences, and $\bar J$ is the average
of their matrix logarithms. In this vignette we illustrate the function and its
methods on simulated data.

\section{Gaussian data}

For bivariate normal data, the directions and the eigenvalues can be compared
with those of the covariance matrix:

<<gauss>>=
library(ospca)
set.seed(1)
Sigma <- matrix(c(4, 1, 1, 1), 2)
Xg <- matrix(rnorm(2000), ncol = 2) %*% chol(Sigma)
fit_g <- gspca(Xg)
fit_g
directions(fit_g)
eigen(Sigma)$vectors
0.5 * log(eigen(Sigma)$values)
@

Note that, as in classical PCA, the signs of the directions are arbitrary.

\section{Nonlinear data}

As a nonlinear example we use observations around a parabola:

<<data>>=
set.seed(1)
x <- rnorm(1000)
X <- cbind(x1 = x, x2 = x^2 + rnorm(1000, sd = 0.5))
fit <- gspca(X)
summary(fit)
@

The default grid has about two observations per grid point, here $22^2$ grid
points. A few grid points whose Jacobian estimate is not positive definite are
left out of $\bar J$; their proportion is given in the summary and is also
available as \code{fit\$diagnostics\$dropped}.

\code{plot()} draws the data together with the principal curves
$T^{-1}(r u_j)$, and \code{screeplot()} shows the eigenvalues of $\bar J$
(bars) together with the reconstruction errors (line) described in
Section~\ref{sec:reconstruction}; see Figure~\ref{fig:plots}. The first
direction is vertical. Its curve runs up the middle of the parabola and then
into the left arm, as there are no observations in the middle above a certain
height. The second curve follows the bend at the bottom of the parabola. Note
that the eigenvalues of $\bar J$ are on a logarithmic scale and can therefore be
negative; for Gaussian data, a negative eigenvalue corresponds to a variance
below one.

<<plots, fig.height = 5, fig.cap = "Data with the GS-PCA principal curves (left) and scree plot (right).">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2))
plot(fit)
screeplot(fit)
@

The curves are part of the fit (\code{fit\$curves}). They cover only the range
of the reference grid, where $T^{-1}$ is estimated, and they inherit the
roughness of the discrete map. The argument \code{eps\_curves} smooths them by a
Tikhonov filter, which changes only the curves and not the directions
(Figure~\ref{fig:smooth-curves}):

<<smooth-curves, fig.width = 4.5, fig.height = 5, out.width = "0.5\\textwidth", fig.cap = "Principal curves smoothed with \\texttt{eps\\_curves = 1}.">>=
fit_s <- gspca(X, eps_curves = 1)
plot(fit_s)
@

\section{Scores, new observations and reconstruction}\label{sec:reconstruction}

The scores are the coordinates $\langle T(X_i), u_j \rangle$ of the transformed
observations $T(X_i)$, which are also part of the fit (\code{fit\$TX}). Since
the estimated map is discrete, $T(X_i)$ is the (mass-weighted) grid point that
the observation is coupled with.

<<scores>>=
head(scores(fit))
head(fit$TX)
@

\code{predict()} maps new observations to the reference space by the cyclically
monotone extension of the discrete map. The potentials of this extension are
computed with the first call, which therefore takes longer than later ones.

<<predict>>=
Xnew <- rbind(c(-2, 4), c(2, 4), c(0, -0.5))
predict(fit, Xnew)
@

\code{reconstruct()} gives the $k$-dimensional representation, and
\code{reconstruction\_mse()} the mean squared reconstruction error. With the
first direction only, the observations are replaced by points on the first
curve, so that their horizontal position is lost; the second direction adds it:

<<reconstruct>>=
sapply(1:2, function(k) reconstruction_mse(X, reconstruct(fit, n_components = k)))
@

\code{fitted()} and \code{residuals()} give the reconstructions and the data
minus their reconstructions:

<<fitted>>=
head(fitted(fit, n_components = 1))
head(residuals(fit, n_components = 1))
@

\section{Estimation choices}

The estimate of $\bar J$ depends on the grid and on the finite differences. The
argument \code{n\_grid} gives the number of normal quantiles per axis,
\code{step} the number of grid steps over which the central differences are
taken, and \code{boundary} the treatment of the edge of the grid: one-sided
differences (\code{"clamp"}, the default), the Jacobian of the nearest interior
grid point (\code{"nearest"}), or interior grid points only (\code{"trim"}). The
defaults (two grid steps, about two observations per grid point and
\code{"clamp"}) were the most accurate in a simulation with known $\bar J$.
Note that the estimates can depend noticeably on these choices. Leaving out the
edge of the grid (\code{"trim"}), for example, cuts off the normal tails, which
here changes the eigenvalues considerably:

<<choices>>=
gspca(X, step = 1, boundary = "nearest", n_grid = 15)$eigenvalues
gspca(X, boundary = "trim")$eigenvalues
fit$eigenvalues
@

The optimal coupling uses a dense cost matrix of $n$ times $n_g^d$ entries, so
that for large samples a smaller \code{n\_grid} should be chosen.

